@node Bug report,  , Mailing list, Overview
@comment  node-name,  next,  previous,  up
@section Bug report

If you think you find a bug, please send a bug report to
@email{bug-zebra@@gnu.org}.  Zebra maintainers subscribe the list.  When
you send a bug report, please be careful about below points.

@itemize @bullet
@item 
Please note what kind of OS are you using.  If you use IPv6 stack please
note about that.
@item
Please show us the result of @code{netstat -rn} and @code{ifconfig -a}.
Information from zebra's VTY command @code{show ip route} will also
helpful.
@item
Please send your configuration file with the report.  If you specify the
argument to the configure script please note about that.
@end itemize

Bug report is very important for us for improving the quality of Zebra.
Zebra is still in development stage but please don't hesitate to send a
bug report to @email{bug-zebra@@gnu.org}.


@node  Installation, Configuration File, Overview, Top
@comment  node-name,  next,  previous,  up
@chapter Installation

Zebra is in development stage, so there is no official release in the
past.  Zebra's beta package can be found at
@url{ftp://ftp.zebra.org/pub/zebra/}.  Once it is released it will be
found at GNU FTP archive sites.

Zebra installation is very easy by configure script.  It automatically
detects current status of the machine so please check kernel
configuration before run configure script.

@menu
* GNU/Linux configuration::     
* Configure options::           
* VTY port setup::              
@end menu

@node GNU/Linux configuration, Configure options, Installation, Installation
@comment  node-name,  next,  previous,  up
@section GNU/Linux configuration

GNU/Linux has very flexible kernel configuration feature.  If you use
GNU/Linux, make it sure current kernel configuration is what you want.
Zebra will run with any kernel configuration but some recommendation
exists.

@table @var

@item CONFIG_NETLINK
Kernel/User netlink socket.  This is a brand new feature which enable
advanced Kernel between Zebra interface (@pxref{Linux netlink}).

@item CONFIG_RTNETLINK
Routing messages.  This makes it possible netlink routing message.  If
you specify this option, @code{zebra} can detect routing information
updates from the kernel (@pxref{Kernel interface}).

@item CONFIG_IP_MULTICAST
IP: multicasting.  This option should be specified when you use
@code{ripd} or @code{ospfd} because these protocol use multicast.

@end table

IPv6 supported is added from GNU/Linux kernel version 2.2.  If you try
to use Zebra IPv6 feature on GNU/Linux kernel, please make it sure below
library has been installed.

@table @code

@item inet6-apps
@code{inet6-apps} package includes basic IPv6 related library such as
@code{inet_ntop} and @code{inet_pton}.  Some basic IPv6 programs such as
@code{ping}, @code{ftp}, @code{inetd} also included. @code{inet-apps}
can be found at @url{ftp://ftp.inner.net/pub/ipv6/}.

@item net-tools
@code{net-tools} provide IPv6 enabled interface and routing utility.  It
contains @code{ifconfig}, @code{route}, @code{netstat} and other tools.
@code{net-tools} can be found at @url{ftp://somewhere/}.

@end table

@node Configure options, VTY port setup, GNU/Linux configuration, Installation
@comment  node-name,  next,  previous,  up
@section Configure options

Normally Zebra configure detects host configuration automatically.  For
manual configuration there are several configure options.  You can turn
off IPv6 support or disable of making some daemons by configure options.

@table @code
@item --enable-guile
Turn on making zebra-guile interpreter.  You will need guile library to
make this.
@item --disable-ipv6
Turn off IPv6 related features and daemons.
@item --disable-zebra
Do not build zebra daemon.
@item --disable-bgpd
Do not build bgpd.
@item --disable-ripd
Do not build ripd.
@item --disable-ripngd
Do not build ripngd.
@item --disable-ospfd
Do not build ospfd.
@item --disable-ospf6d
Do not build ospf6d.
@item --disable-pthread
Do not use pthread features.  Default is disable so you don't need
specify it.
@end table

You can specify any of above option to the configure script.
Configuration and making program is very simple.  You run
@code{configure} and then @code{make} Zebra:

@example
% ./configure --disable-ipv6
% make
@end example

will create zebra and other beasts.  Installation is done by:

@example
% make install
@end example

This will install all of Zebra software under @var{prefix}/sbin.  Sample
configuration installed under @var{prefix}/etc.  If you want to change
the installation directory.  Please specify below option to the
configure.

@table @code
@item --prefix=@var{prefix}
Install architecture-independent files in @var{prefix} [/usr/local].
@item --sysconfdir=@var{dir}
Read-only sample configuration file in @var{dir} [@var{prefix}/etc].
@end table

@node VTY port setup,  , Configure options, Installation
@comment  node-name,  next,  previous,  up
@section VTY port setup

Zebra daemon's has each own terminal interface which called VTY.  So
after Zebra beasts installation you have to setup each program's port
number for connecting to daemons.  Please add below entry to
@file{/etc/services}.

@example
zebrasrv      2600/tcp		  # zebra service
zebra         2601/tcp		  # zebra vty
ripd          2602/tcp		  # RIPd vty
ripngd        2603/tcp		  # RIPngd vty
ospfd         2604/tcp		  # OSPFd vty
bgpd          2605/tcp		  # BGPd vty
ospf6d        2606/tcp		  # OSPF6d vty
@end example

If you use FreeBSD newer than 2.2.8, above entry is already added to
@file{/etc/services}.  So there is no need of adding.

@node Configuration File, VTY, Installation, Top
@comment  node-name,  next,  previous,  up
@chapter Configuration File

Zebra daemon has it's own configuration file.  For example zebra's
default configuration file name is @file{zebra.conf}.  Program name plus
@file{.conf} is default configuration file name. You can specify
configuration file by -f or --config-file option to the daemon.

In configuration file, you can write debug option, vty's password,
routing daemon configuration, logfile name and so on.

@menu
* Basic configuration::         
* Sample Configuration::        
@end menu

@node Basic configuration, Sample Configuration, Configuration File, Configuration File
@comment  node-name,  next,  previous,  up
@section Basic configuration

@deffn Command {hostname @var{hostname}} {}
Set hostname of the router.
@end deffn

@deffn Command {password @var{password}} {}
Set password for vty interface.  If there is no password vty don't
accept connection.
@end deffn

@deffn Command {enable password @var{password}} {}
Set enable password.
@end deffn

@deffn Command {log stdout} {}
@deffnx Command {no log stdout} {}
Set loggin output to stdout.
@end deffn

@deffn Command {log file @var{filename}} {}
If you want to log into the file please specify @code{filename} as
follow.
@example
log file /usr/local/etc/bgpd.log
@end example
@end deffn

@deffn Command {log syslog} {}
@deffnx Command {no log syslog} {}
Set loggin output to syslog.
@end deffn

@deffn Command {write terminal} {}
Display current configuration to the vty interface.
@end deffn

@deffn Command {write file} {}
Write current configuration to configuration file.
@end deffn

@deffn Command {configure terminal} {}
Change to configuration mode.  This command is first step to
configuration.
@end deffn

@deffn Command {terminal length @var{<0-512>}} {}
Set terminal display length to @var{<0-512>}.  If length is 0 no display
control is done.
@end deffn

@deffn Command {who} {}
@end deffn

@deffn Command {list} {}
List commands.
@end deffn

@deffn Command {service password-encryption} {}
