@node ripd, ripngd, zebra, Top
@comment  node-name,  next,  previous,  up
@chapter ripd

The Routing Information Protocol RIP is legendary protocol.  It is a
very simple distance vector routing protocol. @code{ripd} supports RIP
version 2 described in RFC2453 and RIP version 1 described in RFC1058.
RIP is a "distance-vector" protocol, based on the "Bellman-Ford"
algorithms.  The RIP protocol was developed in the 1970s at Xerox labs
as part of the XNS routing protocol.  As a distance-vector protocol,
routers running RIP will send updates to its neighbors thus allowing the
convergence to a known topology.  In each update, the distance of any
given router will be broadcast to its neighbor.

The following points are important to consider in the implementation of
ripd:

One of the characteristics of ripd used in Zebra is that the user must
have a root privilege.  `ripd' uses port 521, but in order to bind this
port, a privilege is needed.

Netmask of ripd supports both version 1 and version 2 of RIP.  Version 1
of RIP originally contained no netmask information.  In RIP version 1,
classes were originally used to determine the size of the network mask.
Class A networks use 8 bits of mask, Class B networks use 16 bits of
masks, while Class C networks use 24 bits of mask.  Today, the most
widely used method of a network mask is assigned to the packet in the
basis of the interface that received the packet.  Version 2 of RIP
supports a variable length subnet mask (VLSM).  By extending the subnet
mask, the mask can be divided and reused.  Each subnet can be used for
different purposes such as large to middle size LANs and WAN links.
Zebra ripd does not support the non-sequential net masks that are
included in RIP Version 2.

In a case of the same information with the same prefix and metric, the
old information will be suppressed.  Ripd does not currently support
equal cost multipath routing.

