@node ripd configuration, Filtering RIP route, Invoking ripd, ripd
@comment  node-name,  next,  previous,  up
@section ripd configuration

@deffn Command {router rip} {}
The "router rip" command is necessary to enable RIP.  To disable RIP,
use the "no router" command.  RIP must be enabled before carrying out
any of the commands listed below in section 6.2.
@end deffn

RIP can be configured to process either Version 1 or Version 2, but the
default mode is Version 2.  If no version is specified, the RIP will
default to Version 2.  In the case that RIP is set to Version 1, the
setting "Version 1" will be displayed, but the setting "Version 2" will
not be displayed whether or not Version 2 is set explicitly as the
version of RIP being used.

@deffn {RIP Command} {version @var{version}} {}
Set RIP process's version.  @var{version} can be `1' or `2'.
@end deffn



@deffn {RIP Command} {network @var{network}} {}
@deffnx {RIP Command} {no network @var{network}} {}
Set RIP enable interface by @var{network}.  The interfaces which has
address matched to @var{network} is enabled.

This group of commands either enables or disables RIP interfaces between
certain numbers of a specified network address.  For example, if network
for 10.0.0.0/24 is enabled for RIP, this would result in all the
addresses from 10.0.0.0 to 10.0.0.255 being enabled for RIP.  The no
network command will disable RIP for the specified network.
@end deffn

@deffn {RIP Command} {network @var{ifname}} {}
@deffnx {RIP Command} {no network @var{ifname}} {}
Set RIP enable interface by @var{ifname}.  Both the sending and
receiving of RIP will be enabled only on the on the port specified in
the network ifname command.  The "no network ifname" command will
disable RIP on the specified inferface name
@end deffn

@deffn {RIP Command} {neighbor @var{a.b.c.d}} {}
@deffnx {RIP Command} {no neighobr @var{a.b.c.d}} {}
Specify RIP neighbor.  This command is used when a neighbor doesn't
understand multicast to specify a neighbor.  In some cases, not all
routers will be able to understand multicasting, where packets are sent
to network or group addresses.  In a situation where a neighbor can not
process multicast routing, it is necessary to establish a direct link
between router and router.  The neighbor command allows the network
administrator to specify a router as a RIP neighbor though its address.
The no neighbor a.b.c.d. command will disable the RIP neighbor.
@end deffn

How to announce RIP route.

@deffn {RIP commnad} {redistribute static} {}
@deffnx {RIP command} {no redistribute static} {}
Redistribute static redistributes routing information from a static route into RIP. No redistribute static disables the route.
@end deffn

@deffn {RIP commnad} {redistribute connected} {}
@deffnx {RIP commnad} {no redistribute connected} {}
Redistribute a connected route into the RIP domain.  No redistribute
connected disables the connected route in the RIP domain.
@end deffn

If you want to specify RIP only static route.

@deffn {RIP command} {route @var{a.b.c.d/m}} {}
@deffnx {RIP command} {no route @var{a.b.c.d/m}} {}
This command is specific to Zebra.  The route command makes a static
route only inside RIP. This command should be used only be advanced
users who are particularly knowledgeable about the RIP protocol.  In
most cases, we reccomend creating a static route in Zebra and
redistributing it in RIP using 'redistribute static.'
@end deffn

There is four timers in RIP.  One is

@deffn {RIP command} {timers basic @var{update} @var{invalid} @var{holddown} @var{flush}} {}

The default settings for the timers are as follows Update timer is 30
seconds.  The invalid timer is 180 seconds after which a route will be
marked as invalid.  The holddown timer is 180 seconds.  During holddown,
new routes will not be instituted after a route is considered invalid.
The flush command is 240 seconds, after which a route will be removed
from the routing table after being declared invalid.

The 'timers basic' command allows the the default values of the timers
listed above to be changed.
@end deffn

@deffn {RIP command} {no timers basic} {}
The 'no timers basic' command will reset the timers to default settings
listed above.
@end deffn

@deffn {Interface command} {ip rip send version @var{varsion}} {}
@var{varsion} can be `1', `2', `1 2'.  This configuration override
router rip's version setting.  This command will enable the selected
interface to send packets with RIP Version 1, RIP Version 2, or both.
In the case of '1 2', packets will be both broadcast and multicast.
@end deffn

@deffn {Interface command} {ip rip receive version @var{varsion}} {}
Version setting for incoming RIP packet.  This command will enable the
selected interface to receive packets in RIP Version 1, RIP Version 2,
or both.
@end deffn

@node  Filtering RIP route, ripd terminal mode commands, ripd configuration, ripd
@comment  node-name,  next,  previous,  up
@section Filtering RIP route

RIP routes can be filtered by distribute-list.

@deffn Command {distribute-list @var{access_list} @var{direct} @var{ifname}} {}
You can apply access list to the interface by distribute-list command.
@var{access_list} is access list name.  @var{direct} is @samp{in} or
@samp{out}.  If @var{direct} is @samp{in} access list is applied to
input packet.

The distribute-list command can be used to filter the RIP path.
Distribute-list can apply access-list to a chosen interface.  First, one
should specify the access-list.  Next, the name of the access list is
used in the distribute-list command.  For example, in the following
configuration eth0 will permit only the paths that match the route
10.0.0.0/8

@example
@group
!
access-list private permit 10 10.0.0.0/8
access-list private deny any
!
distribute-list private in eth0
!
@end group
@end example
@end deffn

Distribute list can be applied both to incoming and outgoing directions.

