@comment  node-name,  next,  previous,  up
@section Invoking bgpd

@code{bgpd} specific invoking options.  Common options also can be
specified (@pxref{Common invoking options}).

@table @samp

@item -p @var{PORT}
@itemx --bgp_port=@var{PORT}
Set bgp protocol's port number.

@item -r
@itemx --retain
When program terminates, retain added route by zebra.

@end table

@node bgpd configuration, bgpd terminal mode commands, Invoking bgpd, bgpd
@comment  node-name,  next,  previous,  up
@section bgpd configuration

There is some commands to configure bgpd.  Default configuration file is
@file{bgpd.conf}.

@deffn Command {router zebra} {}
With this statement, BGP routes go to the @code{zebra} daemon.  If you
want to install BGP routes into the kernel.  You have to specify this
command.
@end deffn

@deffn Command {router bgp @var{as-number}} {}
Enable BGP protocol process with specified @var{as-number}.  After this
statement you can input @code{BGP Command} listed below.
@end deffn

In @code{router bgp} clause there are neighbor specific configuration.

@deffn {BGP Command} {bgp router-id @var{router-id}} {}
This command specify router ID of the router.  If bgpd connect to zebra
it gets interface and address information.  In that case default router
ID value is selected as the largest IP Address of the interfaces.  When
@code{router zebra} is not enabled bgpd can't get interface information
so @code{router-id} is set to 0.0.0.0.  So please set router-id by
yourself.
@end deffn

@deffn {BGP Command} {network @var{network}} {}
This command add the announcement network.
@example
@group
router bgp 1
 network 10.0.0.0/8
@end group
@end example
This configuration means network 10.0.0.0/8 will be announced to the all
neighbors.  Some vendor router don't advertize routes which doesn't
exist in IGP routing table but bgp doesn't care about IGP routing table
when announce the route.
@end deffn

@deffn {BGP Command} {aggregate-address @var{network}} {}
This command specify aggregate address.
@end deffn

@deffn {BGP Command} {neighbor @var{peer} remote-as @var{as-number}} {}
Create new neighbor as remote-as is @var{as-number}.  @var{peer} can be
IPv4 address or IPv6 address.
@example
@group
router bgp 1
 neighbor 10.0.0.1 remote-as 2
@end group
@end example
In this case my router try to peer with AS2.

This command must be the first command to configure neighbor.  If
remote-as is not specified, bgpd will complain like this
@example
can't find neighbor 10.0.0.1
@end example
@end deffn

@deffn {BGP Command} {neighbor @var{peer} next-hop-self} {}
This command specify announce route's nexthop to self address.
@end deffn

@deffn {BGP Command} {neighbor @var{peer} weight @var{weight}} {}
This command specify default @var{weight} value of the neigbor's route.
@end deffn

@deffn {BGP Command} {neighbor @var{peer} default-originate} {}
In default, bgpd does not announce default route (0.0.0.0/0) even it is
in routing table.  When you want to announce default route to the peer,
please specify this command.
@end deffn

@deffn {BGP Command} {neighbor @var{peer} shutdown} {}
@deffnx {BGP Command} {no neighbor @var{peer} shutdown} {}
Shutdown the peer.  We can delete the neighbor's configuration by
@code{no neighbor @var{peer} remote-as @var{as-number}} but all the
configuration of the neighbor will be deleted.  When one want to
preserve the configuration but want to drop BGP peer, please use this
command.
@end deffn

@deffn {BGP Command} {neighbor @var{peer} description ...} {}
@deffnx {BGP Command} {no neighbor @var{peer} description ...} {}
Set description to the peer.
@end deffn

@deffn {BGP Command} {neighbor @var{peer} distribute-list @var{direct}} {}
This command specify distribute-list to the peer.  @var{direct} is
@samp{in} or @samp{out}.
@end deffn

@deffn {BGP Command} {neighbor @var{peer} route-map @var{direct}} {}
Apply route-map to the neighbor.  @var{direct} must be @code{in} or
@code{out}.
@end deffn

@deffn {BGP Command} {neighbor @var{peer} version @var{version}} {}
Set up neighbor's BGP version.  @var{version} can be @var{4}, @var{4+}
or @var{4-}.  BGP version @var{4} is default value of the BGP peering.
BGP version @var{4+} means that the neighbor supports Multiprotocol
Extensions for BGP-4.  BGP version @var{4-} is similar but the neighbor
speaks old Internet-Draft revision 00's Multiprotocol Extensions for
BGP-4.  Some routing software is still using this version.
@end deffn

@deffn {BGP Command} {neighbor @var{peer} ebgp-multihop} {}
@end deffn

@deffn {BGP Command} {neighbor @var{peer} interface @var{ifname}} {}
When you connect BGP peer over IPv6 link-local address.  You have to
specify ifname of the interface.
@end deffn

