@node Kernel interface, Linux netlink, IPv6 supports, Top
@comment  node-name,  next,  previous,  up
@chapter Kernel interface

There are several different method to read kernel routing table
information, update kernel routing table, and look up interfaces.

@table @samp

@item ioctl
@samp{ioctl} method is very traditional way for reading/writing kernel
information.  @samp{ioctl} can be used for looking up interface and
get/set interface address, flags, mtu and other information.  Also
@samp{ioctl} can insert and delete kernel routing table information.  It
will available on almost platform which zebra supports but it is little
bit ugly method so if more better method is supported by the kernel
zebra uses that.

@item sysctl
@samp{sysctl} can lookup kernel information by MIB (Management
Information Base) syntax.  Normally it only provide a way of getting
information from the kernel.  So one want to change kernel information
the other method such as @samp{ioctl} will be required.

@item proc filesystem
@samp{proc filesystem} provide easy way of getting kernel information.

@item routing socket

@item netlink

@end table

@node Linux netlink, Command Index, Kernel interface, Top
@comment  node-name,  next,  previous,  up
@chapter Linux netlink

On recent Linux kernel (2.0.x and 2.2.x), there is a kernel/user
communication support which called @code{netlink}.  It can make it
possible asynchronous communication between kernel and Zebra such like
routing socket over BSD systems.

Before you use this feature, you are sure to select kernel support
option 'Kernel/User network link driver' and 'Routing messages'.

Today, /dev/route special device file is obsolete.  Netlink
communication is done by read/write over netlink socket.

After the kernel configuration, please reconfigure and make install
Zebra.  You can use netlink as a dynamic routing update channel between
Zebra and kernel.

